@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2188 = private unnamed_addr constant [22 x i8] c"for-loop-set-mutations", align 1
@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2189 = private unnamed_addr constant [776 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for code that updates a set with the contents of an iterable by\0Ausing a `for` loop to call `.add()` or `.discard()` on each element\0Aseparately.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen adding or removing a batch of elements to or from a set, it's more\0Aidiomatic to use a single method call rather than adding or removing\0Aelements one by one.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0As = set()\0A\0Afor x in (1, 2, 3):\0A    s.add(x)\0A\0Afor x in (1, 2, 3):\0A    s.discard(x)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0As = set()\0A\0As.update((1, 2, 3))\0As.difference_update((1, 2, 3))\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThe fix will be marked as unsafe if applying the fix would delete any comments.\0AOtherwise, it is marked as safe.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `set`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#set)\0A", align 1
